1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:16,488
On the morning of the eleventh of September 1973, jets of the Chilean air force fired rockets into La Moneda, the presidential palace in Santiago. Inside, the elected president refused to surrender.

2
00:00:17,588 --> 00:00:25,929
By the afternoon he was dead, and the army commander he had appointed less than three weeks earlier was in charge of the country.

3
00:00:27,029 --> 00:00:33,024
That commander was Augusto Pinochet. He would rule Chile for the next seventeen years.

4
00:00:34,124 --> 00:00:48,460
Chile's official commissions later recognised more than forty thousand victims of his regime. Over three thousand were killed or disappeared. Tens of thousands more were imprisoned and tortured for their politics.

5
00:00:49,560 --> 00:01:02,360
To millions he was a dictator who murdered his opponents. To others he was the man who saved Chile from communism and rebuilt its economy. This is the story of both reputations.

6
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:15,056
Augusto Pinochet was born on the twenty-fifth of November, 1915, in the port city of Valparaíso, into a middle-class family.

7
00:01:16,156 --> 00:01:28,615
He entered the military academy as a young man and spent the next four decades rising steadily through the army. Colleagues saw him as dutiful, cautious and uninterested in politics.

8
00:01:29,715 --> 00:01:40,509
He taught at the war academy, wrote books on geography and geopolitics, and by 1971 commanded the army garrison in Santiago.

9
00:01:42,709 --> 00:01:54,208
In 1970, Chileans elected Salvador Allende, a Marxist doctor, as president. He won just over a third of the vote, and Congress confirmed him.

10
00:01:55,308 --> 00:02:08,897
Allende nationalised the copper mines and the banks and broke up large estates. His supporters saw a peaceful, democratic road to socialism. His opponents feared Chile was becoming another Cuba.

11
00:02:09,997 --> 00:02:27,789
So did the United States. In 1970, according to the notes of the CIA's director, President Richard Nixon ordered the agency to make Chile's economy scream. Declassified documents show Washington worked against Allende from the start.

12
00:02:28,889 --> 00:02:41,071
By 1973 Chile was in crisis. Inflation was running at hundreds of percent, shops were short of food, and strikes and street clashes divided the nation.

13
00:02:42,171 --> 00:02:56,549
On the twenty-third of August 1973, Allende made Pinochet commander of the army, believing him loyal. Only days before the coup did Pinochet join the officers plotting to overthrow him.

14
00:02:58,749 --> 00:03:05,875
On the eleventh of September, the armed forces moved. Tanks surrounded the palace, and the air force bombed it.

15
00:03:06,975 --> 00:03:21,012
Allende gave a final, defiant radio address. Then, as soldiers entered the building, he shot himself. A forensic investigation in 2011 confirmed that his death was suicide.

16
00:03:22,112 --> 00:03:32,885
A four-man junta took control, led by Pinochet as head of the army. Within fifteen months he had pushed the others aside and taken the title of president.

17
00:03:33,985 --> 00:03:42,433
Congress was closed. Left-wing parties were banned and the rest suspended. Newspapers were censored and a curfew imposed.

18
00:03:44,633 --> 00:03:53,231
In the weeks after the coup, thousands of people were rounded up. The National Stadium in Santiago became a mass detention camp.

19
00:03:54,331 --> 00:04:10,629
Among those killed was Víctor Jara, a much-loved folk singer, who was detained at another Santiago stadium and shot days after the coup. In 2018, a Chilean court convicted retired army officers of his murder.

20
00:04:11,729 --> 00:04:25,063
In October, a group of army officers toured the provinces by helicopter. In what became known as the Caravan of Death, dozens of political prisoners were taken from their cells and executed.

21
00:04:26,163 --> 00:04:41,672
In 1974 the regime formalised a secret police, known as DINA, under Colonel Manuel Contreras, who reported to Pinochet. It ran secret torture centres, such as Villa Grimaldi in Santiago.

22
00:04:42,772 --> 00:04:53,353
DINA also helped build Operation Condor, a secret network of South American military governments that tracked, seized and killed each other's exiles.

23
00:04:54,453 --> 00:05:03,179
Many prisoners were never seen again. Their families called them the disappeared. For years, the state denied knowing anything about them.

24
00:05:04,279 --> 00:05:25,868
DINA reached abroad too. In 1974 a car bomb in Buenos Aires killed General Carlos Prats, Pinochet's predecessor as army chief, and his wife. In 1976 a bomb in Washington killed the exiled former minister Orlando Letelier and his young American colleague, Ronni Moffitt.

25
00:05:28,068 --> 00:05:43,257
Pinochet handed the economy to a group of economists trained at the University of Chicago, known as the Chicago Boys. They cut spending, sold state companies, opened Chile to trade and later privatised pensions.

26
00:05:44,357 --> 00:05:53,360
In 1982 the economy crashed. Output fell by roughly fourteen percent, banks failed, and unemployment soared.

27
00:05:54,460 --> 00:06:11,591
The reforms were adjusted, and from the mid-1980s Chile grew fast. Admirers abroad called it an economic miracle. Critics point to deep inequality, and to the fact that it was imposed on a country where nobody could safely object.

28
00:06:13,791 --> 00:06:29,087
In 1980, a new constitution was approved in a plebiscite held without electoral rolls or a free press. It kept Pinochet in power, but promised a yes-or-no vote on his rule in 1988.

29
00:06:30,187 --> 00:06:40,384
Opposition parties, banned or harassed for years, united behind a campaign for No. Its television adverts were cheerful and hopeful, and they worked.

30
00:06:41,484 --> 00:06:55,095
On the fifth of October 1988, about fifty-six percent of voters said no to Pinochet. Other members of the junta made clear they would not help him ignore the result, and he accepted it.

31
00:06:56,195 --> 00:07:11,768
In March 1990 he handed the presidency to an elected civilian, Patricio Aylwin. But he stayed on as head of the army for eight more years, then became a senator for life, protected by parliamentary immunity.

32
00:07:13,968 --> 00:07:27,536
In October 1998, while recovering from surgery in London, Pinochet was arrested on a warrant from a Spanish judge, Baltasar Garzón, who wanted to try him for torture and murder.

33
00:07:28,636 --> 00:07:38,364
Britain's highest court ruled that a former head of state had no immunity from extradition for torture. It was a landmark in international law.

34
00:07:39,464 --> 00:07:50,472
He spent sixteen months under house arrest outside London. In March 2000, the British government released him on medical grounds, and he flew home to Chile.

35
00:07:52,672 --> 00:08:03,083
Back home, judges stripped him of his immunity, and he was charged in several cases. Each time, his lawyers argued that he was too ill to stand trial.

36
00:08:04,183 --> 00:08:16,428
Then, in 2004, a United States Senate investigation found he had hidden millions of dollars in secret bank accounts. Even some loyal supporters were shaken.

37
00:08:17,528 --> 00:08:27,491
He died on the tenth of December 2006, aged ninety-one. It was International Human Rights Day. He was never convicted of any crime.

38
00:08:28,591 --> 00:08:39,300
Thousands of supporters filed past his coffin. In other streets, people celebrated. He received military honours, but the government refused him a state funeral.

39
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:55,324
Hundreds of former officers and agents have since been charged or convicted. Manuel Contreras, the head of DINA, died in custody in 2015, serving sentences of hundreds of years.

40
00:08:56,424 --> 00:09:16,200
His defenders still credit him with rescuing Chile from chaos and laying the foundations of one of Latin America's strongest economies. Chileans voted down two attempts to replace his constitution, in 2022 and 2023, though it had long since been heavily amended.

41
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:28,500
Pinochet never stood trial for the killings. But his arrest in London changed something. Former rulers can no longer assume that a title will protect them from justice.

42
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:40,309
In Santiago's General Cemetery, a long stone wall carries the names of the dead and the disappeared. Half a century on, some families are still searching.
